Patch 46 (written 2026-10-17). In the series after patch 45.

SDL 3.4.14's SDL_WaitEventTimeoutNS (thirdparty/sdl3/src/events/SDL_events.c) waits on Android in
a loop that calls SDL_PumpEventsInternal(true) and then Android_PumpEvents(delay). Pumping with
true pushes a new poll sentinel each time, and every pushed event sends a wakeup, which on Android
is Android_SendLifecycleEvent(SDL_ANDROID_LIFECYCLE_WAKE). Android_PumpEvents then always found
that wakeup queued and returned at once, so the loop never blocked: on the POCO X7 the SDL thread
that runs the windowed app's message loop (SDL_WaitEvent) took about a full big core the whole
time, as many cycles as the guest thread (simpleperf, 20 s in town). Upstream SDL (main and
release-3.4.x, 2026-10-17) has the same loop. Now the loop pumps without a new sentinel (the one
pushed before the loop is taken out by the first event check, so none is queued while it waits;
the next call pushes one), and its wait is capped at EVENT_POLL_INTERVAL_NS (1 ms), as the other
platforms' polling loop is, so devices read by polling (joysticks through HIDAPI) keep being
updated while it waits. The cap holds even with no device to poll: the thread wakes every 1 ms,
far below the old spin (upstream could use the polling-interval helper, which is not built on
Android). Real events still wake it at once.

Only the Android branch changes; every other platform builds the same code. This is the first
patch to a submodule's file: tools/deps/build_sdk.sh and tools/build-deps/windows.ps1 update
submodules with --force, so a reused work folder starts from SDL's own sources again.

Test: the SDK builds for Android and Linux (the installed libSDL3.a's SDL_WaitEventTimeoutNS has
the 1 ms wait). On the POCO X7, the SDL thread's CPU in town and the guest thread's frame times,
before and after (the render agent's simpleperf and thread sampler).

diff --git a/thirdparty/sdl3/src/events/SDL_events.c b/thirdparty/sdl3/src/events/SDL_events.c
index a8ac576bc..862f789a7 100644
--- a/thirdparty/sdl3/src/events/SDL_events.c
+++ b/thirdparty/sdl3/src/events/SDL_events.c
@@ -1733,20 +1733,25 @@ bool SDL_WaitEventTimeoutNS(SDL_Event *event, Sint64 timeoutNS)
 
 #ifdef SDL_PLATFORM_ANDROID
     for (;;) {
-        SDL_PumpEventsInternal(true);
+        /* No new poll sentinel here: pushing one sends a wakeup (Android_SendLifecycleEvent(WAKE)),
+         * so Android_PumpEvents() below returned at once and this loop spun a core while waiting.
+         * No sentinel is queued while it waits; the next call pushes one. The wait is capped as the
+         * other platforms' polling loop is, so devices read by polling (joysticks) stay updated.
+         */
+        SDL_PumpEventsInternal(false);
 
         if (SDL_PeepEvents(event, 1, SDL_GETEVENT, SDL_EVENT_FIRST, SDL_EVENT_LAST) > 0) {
             return true;
         }
 
-        Uint64 delay = -1;
+        Sint64 delay = EVENT_POLL_INTERVAL_NS;
         if (timeoutNS > 0) {
             Uint64 now = SDL_GetTicksNS();
             if (now >= expiration) {
                 // Timeout expired and no events
                 return false;
             }
-            delay = (expiration - now);
+            delay = (Sint64)SDL_min((expiration - now), (Uint64)delay);
         }
         Android_PumpEvents(delay);
     }
